# Line-ending policy.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its SYSTEM gitconfig, so on a
# Windows machine every clone and every `git worktree add` rewrites text files
# to CRLF on checkout. For shell scripts that is fatal, not cosmetic: a CRLF
# shebang makes the kernel look for an interpreter literally named `sh\r`, and
# any non-MSYS shell (WSL bash, dash, busybox) then fails with an error that
# points nowhere near the real cause. Git Bash happens to tolerate it, which is
# what makes this bug so slippery - it reproduces only under some shells.
#
# Pinning `eol=lf` keeps these files LF in the working tree regardless of the
# user's core.autocrlf, so `scripts/pi-review` is executable straight out of a
# fresh worktree with no manual CRLF-stripping step.
*.sh        text eol=lf
scripts/*   text eol=lf
